.TH GETUID 2 "February 2026" "Substrate" "System Calls Manual"
.SH NAME
getuid, geteuid, getgid, getegid, setuid, setgid \- get or set user and group identity
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <unistd.h>
.br
.B #include <sys/types.h>
.PP
.B uid_t getuid(void);
.br
.B uid_t geteuid(void);
.br
.B gid_t getgid(void);
.br
.B gid_t getegid(void);
.PP
.BI "int setuid(uid_t " uid ");"
.br
.BI "int setgid(gid_t " gid ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR getuid ()
returns the real user ID of the calling process.
.PP
.BR geteuid ()
returns the effective user ID of the calling process.
.PP
.BR getgid ()
returns the real group ID of the calling process.
.PP
.BR getegid ()
returns the effective group ID of the calling process.
.PP
.BR setuid ()
sets the effective user ID of the calling process.  If the calling
process is privileged (i.e., its real user ID is 0), the real and saved
set-user-ID are also set.
.PP
.BR setgid ()
sets the effective group ID of the calling process.  If the calling
process is privileged (i.e., its real user ID is 0), the real and saved
set-group-ID are also set.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, these functions return the requested ID or 0.  On error,
\-1 is returned, and
.I errno
is set appropriately.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EPERM
The calling process is not privileged and tried to change its IDs to
values it is not allowed to change them to.
.SH CONFORMING TO
POSIX.1-2001, POSIX.1-2008, 4.3BSD, SVr4.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR getpid (2),
.BR setreuid (2),
.BR setresuid (2)
